## Proof

We prove that $f(t,x) \equiv 0$ on $I \times \overline{\Omega}$.

**Setup.** Let $U := (t_0, t_1) \times \Omega$, an open subset of $\mathbb{R}^{n+1}$. The hypothesis is
$$\int_{I}\int_{\Omega} f(t,x)\,\psi(t,x)\,dx\,dt = 0 \quad \text{for every smooth } \psi \text{ with } \psi(t_0,\cdot)=\psi(t_1,\cdot)=0,\;\; \psi|_{\partial\Omega}=0.$$

**Key observation.** Every function $\psi \in C_c^{\infty}(U)$ (smooth with compact support strictly inside $U$) is admissible. Indeed, if $\operatorname{supp}\psi \subset U$ is compact, then $\psi$ vanishes identically in a neighborhood of $\partial U = (\{t_0,t_1\}\times\Omega)\cup(I\times\partial\Omega)$, so in particular $\psi(t_0,\cdot)=\psi(t_1,\cdot)=0$ and $\psi|_{\partial\Omega}=0$. Therefore the hypothesis implies the stronger statement
$$\int_{U} f\,\psi\,d(t,x) = 0 \quad \text{for all } \psi \in C_c^{\infty}(U). \tag{$\star$}$$

**Fundamental lemma argument.** Suppose, for contradiction, that $f$ is not identically zero on $U$. Then there exists a point $p^* = (t^*, x^*) \in U$ with $f(p^*) \neq 0$. Without loss of generality assume $f(p^*) > 0$ (otherwise replace $f$ by $-f$ in the argument, or apply the argument to $-f$ which also satisfies $(\star)$). Since $f$ is smooth, it is continuous, so there exists $\varepsilon > 0$ and a constant $c > 0$ such that the closed ball $\overline{B_{\varepsilon}(p^*)} \subset U$ and
$$f(t,x) \geq c > 0 \quad \text{for all } (t,x) \in \overline{B_{\varepsilon}(p^*)}.$$

Choose a standard bump function $\psi_0 \in C_c^{\infty}(B_{\varepsilon}(p^*))$ with $\psi_0 \geq 0$ everywhere and $\psi_0 > 0$ on a nonempty open sub-ball, so that $\int_{U} \psi_0\,d(t,x) > 0$. Then $\psi_0$ is admissible, and
$$0 = \int_{U} f\,\psi_0\,d(t,x) = \int_{B_{\varepsilon}(p^*)} f\,\psi_0\,d(t,x) \geq c \int_{B_{\varepsilon}(p^*)} \psi_0\,d(t,x) = c\int_{U}\psi_0\,d(t,x) > 0,$$
a contradiction. The case $f(p^*) < 0$ is handled identically (using $-f$, or by the symmetric inequality $\int f\psi_0 \leq -c\int\psi_0 < 0$). Hence no such $p^*$ exists, and
$$f \equiv 0 \quad \text{on } U = (t_0,t_1)\times\Omega.$$

**Extension to the boundary.** Since $f$ is smooth on $I \times \overline{\Omega}$, it is continuous there. The interior $U$ is dense in $I \times \overline{\Omega}$, and $f$ vanishes on $U$. By continuity, $f$ vanishes on the closure:
$$f(t,x) = 0 \quad \text{for all } (t,x) \in I \times \overline{\Omega}.$$

**Conclusion.** The statement is **true**: under the given hypotheses, $f(t,x) \equiv 0$ on $I \times \overline{\Omega}$. The essential reason is that the admissible class of test functions contains $C_c^{\infty}((t_0,t_1)\times\Omega)$, which is rich enough to localize against any point of the open set via bump functions, forcing $f$ to vanish pointwise by continuity; smoothness then propagates the vanishing to the boundary.

